.TH KILLBILLD 8 "2026-09-09" "killbill-rs 0.1.0" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
killbilld \- event-driven USB-kill daemon
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B killbilld
.RB [ \-c
.IR PATH ]
.RB [ \-s
.IR PATH ]
.br
.B killbilld
.RB [ \-h | \-V ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B killbilld
watches USB add and remove events on the kernel
.B NETLINK_KOBJECT_UEVENT
socket. While
.B armed
and an unauthorized device change occurs, it powers the machine off immediately
so that a LUKS-encrypted disk re-locks and its contents become inaccessible.
.PP
The daemon is the single authority: it owns the configuration, the armed state,
and all writes to the config file. The
.BR killbillctl (1)
CLI and
.BR killbill-tui (1)
client drive it over a local
.B SOCK_SEQPACKET
Unix socket; they never edit the config directly.
.PP
.B killbilld
does not fork or daemonize. Run it under systemd (see
.B FILES
below for the shipped unit). It reads its config, starts the USB sensor and the
control server, and \(en only if
.B armed_at_boot
is set \(en arms.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-config " " \fIPATH\fR
Configuration file. Default:
.IR /etc/killbill/config.toml .
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-socket " " \fIPATH\fR
Control socket path. Default:
.IR /run/killbilld.sock .
The daemon refuses to start if the socket's parent directory is writable by
group or other without the sticky bit set: the socket's mode and owner are
applied by path after
.BR bind (2),
so a writable parent is a symlink-swap window on that step.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print help and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print version and exit.
.SH ARMING AND DISARMING
Arming and disarming are
.B control-socket commands
.RB ( "killbillctl arm" ", " "killbillctl disarm" "), never signals."
This is deliberate: the original
.I usbkill
could be disarmed by anyone able to send it
.BR SIGTERM .
Here, a signal means only "stop the process" and never lowers protection.
.PP
Arming runs a preflight and
.B refuses
(loudly, leaving the daemon disarmed) if it cannot guarantee the kill path:
an invalid config, a USB sensor that will not start, a reported event gap under
.BR "on_sensor_gap = \(dqwarn\(dq" ,
or a missing
.B CAP_SYS_BOOT
capability.
.SH SIGNALS
.TP
.BR SIGTERM ", " SIGINT
Shut the process down. Any USB event already queued is evaluated first, so a
signal racing an unplug still fires. Neither signal disarms.
.PP
If a poweroff is already in flight when a signal or shutdown arrives, the
process
.B parks and does not exit
\(en the machine is going down. The shipped systemd unit sets
.B SendSIGKILL=no
so systemd cannot cut this short.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Clean shutdown.
.TP
.B non-zero
The USB sensor could not be kept alive, or every poweroff path failed with the
machine still up. A supervisor (systemd
.BR Restart=on-failure ) should restart the daemon. Armed state is never
persisted across a restart; only
.B armed_at_boot
re-arms.
.SH SECURITY
The daemon needs
.B CAP_SYS_BOOT
(for
.BR reboot (2))
and write access to
.IR /etc/killbill .
It must
.B not
be granted
.BR CAP_NET_ADMIN :
receiving kernel uevents needs no capability, and holding
.B CAP_NET_ADMIN
would allow forging them. The control socket is mode
.B 0660
owned
.BR root:root .
An invalid configuration disables protection loudly; the daemon never fires on a
half-understood config.
.PP
There is no network activity and no telemetry of any kind. The only IPC is the
local Unix socket.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/killbill/config.toml
Configuration. See
.BR killbill.conf (5).
.TP
.I /run/killbilld.sock
Control socket.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/systemd/system/killbilld.service
The systemd unit (hardened: minimal capability set,
.BR ProtectSystem=full ,
.BR SendSIGKILL=no ).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR killbillctl (1),
.BR killbill-tui (1),
.BR killbill.conf (5),
.BR systemd.exec (5)
.PP
